\section{Discussion: the anatomy of a collapse}

Three independent mechanisms are at work.

\paragraph{(A) The container barrier.}
The factor-dependence is real but confined to the CRT coordinates, and the
coordinate projector is a CRT idempotent, equivalent to the factorisation
(Theorems~\ref{thm:idem} and~\ref{thm:barrier}). This is unconditional. Any
attack of the shape ``extract the coordinate, then exploit the asymmetry'' is
circular.

\paragraph{(B) The invariant barrier.}
Externally, $Q(a)$ is a residue modulo $N$, hence a function of $(N,a)$ alone.
Every multiplicative statistic derived from the power surface factors through
the liar group, whose isomorphism type is $(\Zmod{g})^2$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:liargroup}); semiprimes with equal Euler gap are
indistinguishable by such statistics (Corollary~\ref{cor:indist}).

\paragraph{(C) The reduction barrier.}
The single genuine leak, the gcd variant, has exact reveal count
$g(q-1)+g(p-1)-2g^2$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:reveal}), density $\approx g/p+g/q$: a
$p-1$-smoothness handle, already known.

Together these give the sharpest instance we know of the principle
\emph{asymmetry without the split is invisible}: a quantity may depend genuinely
and asymmetrically on both factors and still yield nothing, because the
dependence is expressed in a coordinate system whose construction is the secret.
The positive reading is that a vague expectation has been converted into exact
arithmetic --- exact liar count, exact liar group, exact reveal count, exact
bijectivity criterion, and an unconditional equivalence between coordinate
reading and factoring.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Euler-gap sufficiency]
Every function of $N$ obtained from the power surface
$\{(a, a^{N-1} \bmod N) : a \in (\Zmod{N})^\times\}$ that is invariant under
multiplicative automorphisms of the unit group is a function of $(N,g)$ only.
\end{conjecture}

The whole surface is the endomorphism $u \mapsto u^{N-1}$, whose kernel is
$(\Zmod{g})^2$ and whose image has size $\varphi(N)/g^2$; an
isomorphism-invariant statistic cannot see more than the isomorphism type. What
remains is to formalise ``isomorphism-invariant statistic'' and derive the
simulation. \emph{Falsifiable:} exhibit such a statistic separating
$33 = 3\cdot 11$ from $35 = 5\cdot 7$, whose liar groups are isomorphic.

\begin{conjecture}[The gap hint is a factoring hint]
Knowing $g = \gcd(p-1,q-1)$ together with $N$ yields the factorisation in
randomised polynomial time whenever $g>1$.
\end{conjecture}

A random unit $u$ has $u^{(N-1)/g'}$ landing in the $g$-torsion for suitable
$g' \mid g$, and $g$-torsion elements that are $1$ in exactly one CRT coordinate
are precisely the nontrivial square roots and idempotents that
Corollaries~\ref{cor:idemsplit} and~\ref{cor:rabin} turn into factors. Both
splits are proved and Theorem~\ref{thm:reveal} gives the exact success count of
the naive sampler; only amplification is open. \emph{Falsifiable in either
direction:} prove the sampling bound, or exhibit a family with $g>1$ whose
induced distribution never leaves the diagonal.

\begin{conjecture}[Hint hierarchy]
Cheap quantities attached to $N$ form a genuine hierarchy by signal content,
with Euler's totient at the top (Theorem~\ref{thm:totient}) and the power
surface at the bottom (a function of $(N,g)$ only), and with intermediate levels
determined by the smallest group-theoretic invariant a hint can express.
\end{conjecture}

Further directions include an unconditional proof that no uniform, hint-free
classical statistic of bounded degree distinguishes semiprimes with equal Euler
gap; a precise comparison with quantum period-finding, which performs exactly
the coordinate read that Theorem~\ref{thm:barrier} forbids classically; and the
analysis of non-invariant aggregations of many weak hints.

\section{Conclusion}

The quantity $Q(a) = a^{N-1} \bmod N$ is cheap, factorisation-free and genuinely
asymmetric: its CRT coordinates are $a^{q-1} \bmod p$ and $a^{p-1} \bmod q$,
each governed by the prime it does not see, and they determine $Q(a)$ uniquely.
Nevertheless $Q$ is factor-blind. Reading a coordinate is unconditionally
equivalent to factoring; every multiplicative statistic collapses onto the Euler
gap $g$, so that semiprimes with equal gap have isomorphic Fermat-liar
structure; and the gcd variant reveals a factor for exactly
$g(q-1)+g(p-1)-2g^2$ bases, a density $\approx g/p+g/q$ that reduces to
classical $p-1$ smoothness. Set against the totient, from which $p$ and $q$
follow in closed form, the boundary is sharp: asymmetry without the split is
invisible.

